<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      net8.0, not net9.0. Nothing in this project needs 9: it is P/Invoke,
      unsafe pointer reads and explicit struct layout, all of which settled
      long before 8. Targeting 9 bought nothing and cost the ability to build
      on a machine whose distribution packages 8, which is a self inflicted
      dependency. 8 is LTS.
    -->
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>disable</ImplicitUsings>
    <EnableNETAnalyzers>true</EnableNETAnalyzers>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>false</GenerateDocumentationFile>

    <!--
      Required by KhzNative.cs. The bindings read cells through pointers into
      the native arena; without unsafe blocks every read would be a marshalled
      copy, which is the opposite of the zero-copy requirement.
    -->
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    No PackageReference, deliberately. sqlite3 is linked by the native library
    from the system, not pulled in here as a NuGet dependency: adding
    Microsoft.Data.Sqlite or SQLitePCLRaw would introduce a second, separate
    copy of sqlite and a managed dependency chain the rules exclude.

    The native library is not referenced through the project file either.
    ProjectReference and PackageReference cannot express "a CMake target built
    out of tree", and inventing a hardcoded path here would break on every
    machine but one. Configure the CMake project in the repository root into a
    build directory, build that directory, then put the resulting
    libkhz_sheet.so, khz_sheet.dll or libkhz_sheet.dylib on the loader path:
    LD_LIBRARY_PATH, the executable's own directory, or a runtimes native
    folder in a published layout.

    The exact command lines are deliberately not written out here. An XML
    comment cannot contain a double hyphen, and this comment previously did -
    it quoted a cmake invocation verbatim, which made the project file
    unparseable and put the whole C# layer out of reach of the compiler with
    error MSB4025. Prose describes the steps; a shell is the place to type
    them.

    KhzAbi.Verify reports ErrMissing when the native library is not found, so
    a missing library is a status code rather than a crash.
  -->

</Project>
